From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libuv: fix uv__hrtime() uint64 overflow on ppc darwin

uv__hrtime() computes time_func() * timebase.numer / timebase.denom.
On Intel/ARM Macs mach_timebase_info returns 1/1 and the multiply is a
no-op, but on PowerPC the timebase ticks at ~33 MHz and numer/denom is
~1000000000/33333333. The product tick_count * 1e9 exceeds 2^64 once
the host has been up for 2^64/numer ticks (~9.2 minutes on a G5), after
which the multiplication wraps mod 2^64 and the "monotonic" clock
becomes a sawtooth that resets to near zero every ~9.2 minutes of host
uptime.

Any node process alive across a wrap sees uv_now() jump backwards,
tripping the CHECK_GE(now, timer_base()) assertion in
node::Environment::GetNow() (env.cc:925) and killing the process with
SIGABRT - this is what aborted "npm install" during the npm6 destroot.
Processes that survive also get broken timers, since every active
timeout suddenly sits ~2^64/numer/1e6 ms in the future.

Fix by splitting the scaling so no intermediate exceeds 2^64:
(t / denom) * numer overflows only after centuries of uptime, and
(t % denom) * numer is at most ~denom * numer ~= 3.3e16 < 2^64. Same
fix as MacPorts applies to the standalone libuv port in
macos-powerpc/powerpc-ports commit d67b01ad; node builds the bundled
libuv 1.40.0 (--shared-libuv is not used), so the bundled copy needs
the fix too.
---
 deps/uv/src/unix/darwin.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/deps/uv/src/unix/darwin.c b/deps/uv/src/unix/darwin.c
index d0ecd452..87b13b0c 100644
--- a/deps/uv/src/unix/darwin.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/unix/darwin.c
@@ -69,8 +69,16 @@ static void uv__hrtime_init_once(void) {
 
 
 uint64_t uv__hrtime(uv_clocktype_t type) {
+  uint64_t t;
   uv_once(&once, uv__hrtime_init_once);
-  return time_func() * timebase.numer / timebase.denom;
+  t = time_func();
+  /* On Intel/ARM Macs the timebase is 1/1, but on PowerPC the timebase
+   * ticks at tens of MHz and numer/denom is ~1000000000/33333333, so the
+   * naive t * numer overflows uint64_t once the host has been up for a few
+   * minutes, wrapping the monotonic clock backwards. Split the scaling so
+   * no intermediate value can exceed 2^64. */
+  return (t / timebase.denom) * timebase.numer
+       + (t % timebase.denom) * timebase.numer / timebase.denom;
 }
 
 
